<body>
  <Header />
  <main>
  <article class="container">
    <header class="article-header">
      <span class="tag">Email Marketing</span>
      <h1>Mailchimp vs Klaviyo: Best Email Marketing 2026</h1>
      <p class="meta">Updated August 2026</p>
    </header>

    <p class="lead">
      Mailchimp and Klaviyo are the two email marketing platforms that most businesses shortlist in 2026, and they serve different needs. Mailchimp wins on ease of use, a free tier, lower entry pricing, and broad integration support for non-ecommerce businesses. Klaviyo wins on ecommerce data depth, revenue-driving automation, predictive analytics, and SMS marketing built in. This comparison breaks down automation, deliverability, segmentation, pricing, SMS, and ecommerce integration so you can pick the right email platform for your business.
    </p>

    <div class="cta-top">
      <a href="https://mailchimp.com" class="btn btn-primary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Mailchimp Free</a>
      <a href="https://www.klaviyo.com" class="btn btn-secondary" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Try Klaviyo Free</a>
    </div>

    <h2>Quick Comparison</h2>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Mailchimp</th><th>Klaviyo</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Best For</td><td>Newsletters, small business, content marketing</td><td>Ecommerce, revenue automation, SMS</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Starting Price</td><td>$13/mo (Essentials), free tier available</td><td>$20/mo (email), $35/mo (email + SMS)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Free Tier</td><td>Yes, 250 contacts, 500 emails/mo</td><td>Yes, 250 profiles, 500 emails/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Ecommerce Focus</td><td>General purpose, ecommerce add-on</td><td>Built for ecommerce from day one</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Automation</td><td>Customer Journey builder</td><td>Flow builder with predictive analytics</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Segmentation</td><td>Basic conditions, pre-built segments</td><td>Deep behavioral, predictive segments</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SMS Marketing</td><td>Add-on, separate pricing</td><td>Built-in, unified with email</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Shopify Integration</td><td>Available, basic sync</td><td>Native, real-time product data</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Product Recommendations</td><td>Limited, manual</td><td>Dynamic, AI-driven</td></tr>
        <tr><td>A/B Testing</td><td>Subject lines, content, send time</td><td>Subject lines, content, send time, flows</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reporting</td><td>Standard email analytics</td><td>Revenue attribution per campaign and flow</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Deliverability</td><td>Good, above industry average</td><td>Strong, optimized for ecommerce</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Mobile App</td><td>iOS, Android</td><td>iOS, Android</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Integrations</td><td>300+ integrations</td><td>350+ integrations, ecommerce focused</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Open Source</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td><td>No, proprietary SaaS</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h2>What Is Mailchimp?</h2>
    <p>Mailchimp is an all-in-one marketing platform founded in 2001 by Ben Chestnut and Dan Kurzius, headquartered in Atlanta, Georgia. Originally built as a side project to help small businesses send email newsletters, Mailchimp has grown into one of the most widely used email marketing platforms in the world, serving over 12 million customers across industries. Mailchimp's core philosophy is that email marketing should be accessible to anyone, which is why it offers a free tier, an intuitive drag-and-drop email builder, and a content studio for designing campaigns without technical skills.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $13 per month for the Essentials plan, with a free tier that supports up to 250 contacts and 500 email sends per month. Mailchimp has expanded beyond email into a full marketing platform with social media advertising, landing pages, postcards, websites, and CRM features. Higher tiers add advanced automation, multivariate testing, and predictive demographics. The defining technical feature is the Customer Journey builder, which lets you create multi-step automation workflows with branching logic. The tradeoff is that Mailchimp's ecommerce features are less deep than Klaviyo, with slower data sync, weaker product recommendations, and less granular revenue attribution.</p>

    <h2>What Is Klaviyo?</h2>
    <p>Klaviyo is a revenue-focused email and SMS marketing platform founded in 2012 by Andrew Bialecki and Ed Hallen, headquartered in Boston, Massachusetts. Unlike Mailchimp, which started as a newsletter tool, Klaviyo was built specifically for ecommerce, with deep integrations to Shopify, WooCommerce, BigCommerce, and Magento from day one. Klaviyo's core philosophy is that email should drive measurable revenue, which is why every campaign and automation is tied to dollar attribution and purchase behavior.</p>
    <p>The current pricing starts at $20 per month for the email plan and $35 per month for the combined email plus SMS plan, and a free plan covers up to 250 profiles with 500 email sends per month. Klaviyo's defining technical feature is its real-time data sync with ecommerce platforms, which captures every product view, add to cart, purchase, and refund. This data powers predictive analytics like customer lifetime value, churn risk, and next order date, which enable revenue-driving automations that Mailchimp cannot match. The tradeoff is that Klaviyo is more expensive at entry and is overkill for businesses that are not selling products online.</p>

    <h2>Automation and Workflows</h2>
    <p>Automation is where the philosophical split between Mailchimp and Klaviyo becomes most visible. Mailchimp's Customer Journey builder is designed for general marketing automation, while Klaviyo's flow builder is purpose-built for ecommerce revenue generation. Both platforms can send automated emails, but the depth and revenue impact differ significantly.</p>

    <h3>Mailchimp Customer Journey Builder</h3>
    <p>Mailchimp's Customer Journey builder uses a visual interface where you define starting points, actions, and branching rules. Common automations include welcome series, abandoned cart, post-purchase follow-up, re-engagement campaigns, and birthday emails. The builder supports conditional logic based on subscriber activity, tags, and merge fields. For small businesses sending newsletters and basic automated sequences, Mailchimp's automation is sufficient and easy to set up without technical skills.</p>
    <p>The limitation is that Mailchimp's ecommerce data sync is slower and less granular than Klaviyo. Abandoned cart emails may trigger with a delay, product recommendations are manual rather than dynamic, and there is no predictive analytics for send time optimization or churn risk. For businesses whose automation needs are primarily content and newsletter focused, Mailchimp is capable. For ecommerce stores that need real-time behavioral triggers, Mailchimp hits a ceiling.</p>

    <h3>Klaviyo Flow Builder</h3>
    <p>Klaviyo's flow builder is one of the most powerful automation engines in email marketing. Flows can trigger on any event captured from your ecommerce platform, including product viewed, added to cart, started checkout, placed order, refunded, and even predictive events like churn risk or next order date. Each flow supports conditional splits based on real-time behavior, time delays, dynamic content blocks with product recommendations, and A/B testing within the flow. The result is revenue-focused automation that adapts to each subscriber's behavior.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo's standout automation features include predictive send time, which uses machine learning to send each email at the time that subscriber is most likely to engage, and predictive churn, which identifies subscribers at risk of lapsing and triggers win-back flows automatically. For ecommerce stores, these features typically generate 20 to 30 percent more email revenue than Mailchimp, according to independent case studies. The tradeoff is that Klaviyo's flow builder has a steeper learning curve, and building sophisticated flows requires understanding ecommerce data models.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for newsletter automation and ease of use goes to <span class="winner-tag">Mailchimp</span>. Best for ecommerce revenue automation and predictive analytics goes to <span class="winner-tag">Klaviyo</span>.</p>

    <h2>Segmentation and Personalization</h2>
    <p>Segmentation is where Klaviyo has a decisive advantage. Klaviyo's segmentation is built on real-time ecommerce data and predictive analytics, while Mailchimp's segmentation is based on subscriber properties and engagement. Both platforms let you target subsets of your audience, but the depth differs significantly.</p>

    <h3>Mailchimp Segmentation</h3>
    <p>Mailchimp lets you create segments based on subscriber activity, tags, merge fields, signup source, and engagement signals like opens and clicks. Pre-built segments make it easy to target engaged subscribers, inactive subscribers, and new signups. For newsletter and content marketing, this level of segmentation is sufficient. The limitation is that Mailchimp cannot segment based on purchase behavior in real time, because its ecommerce data sync is slower and less detailed than Klaviyo.</p>

    <h3>Klaviyo Segmentation</h3>
    <p>Klaviyo lets you create segments based on any data point captured from your ecommerce platform, including products viewed, categories browsed, cart value, order history, customer lifetime value, predicted next order date, and churn risk. You can combine conditions with AND and OR logic to build highly targeted segments. For example, you can create a segment of customers who have spent over $200, viewed a specific product category in the last 7 days, and have a predicted next order date within 30 days. This level of granularity enables personalization that Mailchimp cannot match.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo also supports dynamic content blocks within emails that pull product recommendations based on each subscriber's browsing and purchase history. This means every subscriber sees different product recommendations, which significantly increases click-through and conversion rates.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for basic newsletter segmentation goes to <span class="winner-tag">Mailchimp</span>. Best for ecommerce behavioral segmentation and personalization goes to <span class="winner-tag">Klaviyo</span>.</p>

    <h2>Ecommerce Integration</h2>
    <p>Ecommerce integration is where Klaviyo has a structural advantage. Klaviyo was built for ecommerce, while Mailchimp added ecommerce features as an afterthought. Both platforms integrate with major ecommerce platforms, but the depth and real-time nature of the data differ.</p>

    <h3>Mailchimp Ecommerce</h3>
    <p>Mailchimp integrates with Shopify, WooCommerce, BigCommerce, Magento, and Salesforce Commerce Cloud through official integrations. The integrations sync products, orders, and customers, which enables basic ecommerce automations like abandoned cart and post-purchase follow-up. Mailchimp also supports product recommendations, but they are manual rather than dynamic, meaning you select products to feature rather than having the platform generate recommendations based on subscriber behavior.</p>
    <p>The limitation is that Mailchimp's data sync is not real-time. Abandoned cart emails may trigger with a delay of several minutes to hours, which reduces recovery rates. Product data is also less detailed, which limits the sophistication of behavioral targeting.</p>

    <h3>Klaviyo Ecommerce</h3>
    <p>Klaviyo has native, real-time integrations with Shopify, WooCommerce, BigCommerce, Magento, and custom ecommerce platforms through its API. The integration captures every event, including product viewed, added to cart, started checkout, placed order, refunded, and fulfilled. This real-time data enables automations that trigger within seconds of a behavior, which significantly increases conversion rates for abandoned cart and browse abandonment flows.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo also syncs full product catalogs, which enables dynamic product recommendations based on each subscriber's browsing and purchase history. The platform's revenue attribution tracks exactly how much revenue each campaign and flow generates, which makes it easy to prove ROI to stakeholders.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for basic ecommerce integration goes to <span class="winner-tag">Mailchimp</span>. Best for real-time ecommerce data and revenue attribution goes to <span class="winner-tag">Klaviyo</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pricing Comparison</h2>
    <p>Pricing is where Mailchimp has a significant advantage at the entry level, while Klaviyo offers better ROI for ecommerce stores at scale. The right choice depends on your list size and whether you sell products online.</p>
    <div class="table-wrap">
      <table>
        <tr><th>Tier</th><th>Mailchimp</th><th>Klaviyo</th></tr>
        <tr><td>Free</td><td>250 contacts, 500 emails/mo</td><td>250 profiles, 500 emails/mo</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Entry</td><td>$13/mo (Essentials, 500 contacts)</td><td>$20/mo (Email), $35/mo (Email + SMS)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>1,000 contacts</td><td>$20/mo (Essentials)</td><td>$30/mo (Email), $45/mo (Email + SMS)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>5,000 contacts</td><td>$69/mo (Standard)</td><td>$100/mo (Email), $230/mo (Email + SMS)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>10,000 contacts</td><td>$100/mo (Standard)</td><td>$150/mo (Email), $370/mo (Email + SMS)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>50,000 contacts</td><td>From $350/mo (Premium)</td><td>$700/mo (Email), custom (Email + SMS)</td></tr>
        <tr><td>SMS Included</td><td>No, separate add-on</td><td>Yes, unified with email</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Revenue Attribution</td><td>Basic, on Standard and above</td><td>Full, on all plans</td></tr>
      </table>
    </div>
    <p>For small lists under 500 contacts, Mailchimp is significantly cheaper because of the free tier and $13 Essentials plan. For content creators, bloggers, and small businesses sending newsletters, Mailchimp's pricing is hard to beat. For ecommerce lists above 1,000 contacts, Klaviyo's higher cost is typically justified by the additional revenue it generates. Independent case studies show that ecommerce stores switching from Mailchimp to Klaviyo see a 20 to 40 percent increase in email-attributed revenue, which more than offsets the higher monthly cost.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo bundles SMS into its email plus SMS plan, while Mailchimp charges separately for SMS. For ecommerce stores that want unified email and SMS, Klaviyo's bundled pricing can be cheaper than Mailchimp once you factor in the SMS add-on.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for small lists and tight budgets goes to <span class="winner-tag">Mailchimp</span>. Best for ecommerce ROI and unified email plus SMS goes to <span class="winner-tag">Klaviyo</span>.</p>

    <h2>SMS Marketing</h2>
    <p>SMS marketing is where Klaviyo has a clear advantage. Klaviyo includes SMS in its base pricing with unified email and SMS workflows, while Mailchimp offers SMS as a separate add-on with less integration. Both platforms can send SMS, but the experience differs.</p>
    <p>Klaviyo's SMS is built into the same platform as email, which means you can create flows that combine email and SMS based on subscriber behavior. For example, an abandoned cart flow can send an email after 1 hour, an SMS after 6 hours, and a final email after 24 hours, all within a single flow. SMS analytics are unified with email analytics, so you can see which channel drove the conversion.</p>
    <p>Mailchimp's SMS is available as an add-on in the United States and a few other countries. The SMS tool is functional but less integrated, with separate reporting and no unified flow builder. For ecommerce stores where SMS is a growing revenue channel, Klaviyo's unified approach is significantly better.</p>
    <p><strong>Winner:</strong> Best for unified email and SMS marketing goes to <span class="winner-tag">Klaviyo</span>. Best for email-only needs goes to <span class="winner-tag">Mailchimp</span>.</p>

    <h2>Pros and Cons</h2>

    <h3>Mailchimp Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Free tier for up to 250 contacts</li>
        <li>Lowest entry pricing at $13/mo</li>
        <li>Intuitive drag-and-drop email builder</li>
        <li>300+ integrations across industries</li>
        <li>Built-in landing pages and websites</li>
        <li>Social media advertising included</li>
        <li>Excellent for newsletters and content marketing</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Mailchimp Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Ecommerce data sync is slower than Klaviyo</li>
        <li>Limited predictive analytics</li>
        <li>SMS is a separate add-on, not unified</li>
        <li>Product recommendations are manual</li>
        <li>Revenue attribution less granular</li>
        <li>Pricing jumps at higher contact tiers</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Klaviyo Pros</h3>
    <div class="pros-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Built specifically for ecommerce</li>
        <li>Real-time data sync with Shopify, WooCommerce, BigCommerce</li>
        <li>Predictive analytics for send time, churn, and lifetime value</li>
        <li>Dynamic product recommendations based on behavior</li>
        <li>SMS marketing included in base pricing</li>
        <li>Full revenue attribution per campaign and flow</li>
        <li>Deep segmentation based on purchase history</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h3>Klaviyo Cons</h3>
    <div class="cons-box">
      <ul>
        <li>Free plan capped at 250 profiles</li>
        <li>Email + SMS bundles cost more than email-only rivals</li>
        <li>Overkill for non-ecommerce businesses</li>
        <li>Steeper learning curve for sophisticated flows</li>
        <li>Fewer non-ecommerce integrations</li>
        <li>No built-in landing page builder or website tool</li>
      </ul>
    </div>

    <h2>Who Should Use Which?</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Ecommerce Stores</h3>
      <p>If you sell products online through Shopify, WooCommerce, BigCommerce, or Magento, Klaviyo is the better choice. The real-time data sync, predictive analytics, dynamic product recommendations, and unified SMS marketing are purpose-built for driving ecommerce revenue. Stores doing over $100,000 per year typically see Klaviyo pay for itself within the first month through increased email-attributed revenue.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Content Creators and Bloggers</h3>
      <p>If you run a blog, podcast, or content business and your primary email goal is sending newsletters, Mailchimp is the better choice. The free tier, intuitive builder, and lower pricing make it ideal for content creators who do not need ecommerce data depth. Mailchimp's content studio and landing page builder also reduce the need for separate tools. If you outgrow it, our <a href="/alternatives/mailchimp-alternatives/">Mailchimp alternatives</a> roundup covers other options.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Small Businesses on a Budget</h3>
      <p>If you have under 500 contacts and a tight budget, Mailchimp's free tier is the clear choice. You can send up to 500 emails per month to 250 contacts at no cost, with access to the email builder and basic automation. Klaviyo also has a free plan for up to 250 profiles, but Mailchimp's lower paid tiers make it the stronger zero-budget option.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>B2B Companies and Service Businesses</h3>
      <p>If you sell services rather than products and need email for lead nurturing and client communication, Mailchimp is the better choice. Its CRM features, landing pages, and broader integration support fit B2B workflows better than Klaviyo's ecommerce-focused toolset. Klaviyo's predictive analytics and product recommendations are wasted on service businesses.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Stores Using SMS Marketing</h3>
      <p>If SMS is a growing channel for your store, Klaviyo is the better choice. Its unified email and SMS platform lets you build flows that combine both channels based on subscriber behavior, with unified analytics. Mailchimp's SMS add-on is less integrated and only available in select countries, making Klaviyo the stronger choice for multi-channel messaging.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Real-World Use Cases</h2>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Sending a weekly newsletter to 2,000 subscribers</h3>
      <p>A blogger with 2,000 subscribers sends a weekly newsletter with curated content. Mailchimp Standard at $30 per month handles the list, provides an intuitive email builder, and includes basic automation for welcome sequences. The blogger does not need ecommerce data or SMS. Klaviyo at $100 per month for the same list would be overkill and more expensive, making Mailchimp the right choice for content newsletters.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Recovering abandoned carts for a $200k/mo Shopify store</h3>
      <p>A Shopify store doing $200,000 per month needs to recover abandoned carts, send browse abandonment flows, and run post-purchase upsells. Klaviyo at $230 per month for 5,000 contacts with email and SMS handles all of this with real-time data sync and dynamic product recommendations. The store attributes $40,000 per month in revenue to Klaviyo flows, a 170x ROI. Mailchimp could send abandoned cart emails but with slower sync and no dynamic recommendations, making Klaviyo the clear choice for serious ecommerce.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Building a welcome series for a SaaS company</h3>
      <p>A SaaS company wants to send a 5-email welcome series to new trial users, with branching based on feature usage. Mailchimp's Customer Journey builder handles this with conditional logic based on subscriber activity and tags. The company integrates Mailchimp with its app via API to trigger emails based on user behavior. Klaviyo could do this too but is optimized for ecommerce data rather than SaaS usage data, making Mailchimp the more natural fit for SaaS onboarding.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="use-case">
      <h3>Running a holiday SMS campaign for a fashion store</h3>
      <p>A fashion store wants to run a Black Friday SMS campaign alongside email. Klaviyo's unified platform lets the team build a flow that sends a teaser email on Wednesday, an SMS on Friday morning, and a final email on Friday afternoon, all within one workflow with unified analytics. The store sees a 25x ROI on the SMS campaign. Mailchimp's separate SMS add-on cannot build unified flows, making Klaviyo the necessary choice for integrated email and SMS campaigns.</p>
    </div>

    <h2>Final Verdict</h2>
    <div class="verdict">
      <p><strong>Best for ecommerce stores: Klaviyo.</strong> Klaviyo's real-time ecommerce data sync, predictive analytics, dynamic product recommendations, and unified SMS marketing make it the best choice for stores selling products online. Email plans start at $20 per month ($35 with SMS), which is higher than Mailchimp, but independent case studies show Klaviyo generates 20 to 40 percent more email revenue for ecommerce stores, more than offsetting the price difference.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for newsletters and small business: Mailchimp.</strong> Mailchimp's free tier, $13 Essentials plan, intuitive email builder, and 300+ integrations make it the best choice for content creators, bloggers, service businesses, and small companies that need affordable email marketing without ecommerce complexity. The free tier for 250 contacts is unmatched in the industry.</p>
      <p><strong>Best for SMS marketing: Klaviyo.</strong> Klaviyo includes SMS in its base pricing with unified email and SMS workflows, while Mailchimp charges separately for a less integrated SMS add-on. For stores where SMS is a growing channel, Klaviyo's unified approach is significantly better.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommended approach:</strong> Start on Mailchimp if you are a content creator, blogger, service business, or small company with under 500 contacts and a tight budget. Start on Klaviyo if you run an ecommerce store, need real-time behavioral automation, or want unified email and SMS marketing. Most ecommerce stores switch from Mailchimp to Klaviyo once they exceed $100,000 in annual revenue, because the additional email revenue justifies the higher cost. Most non-ecommerce businesses stay on Mailchimp indefinitely because it meets their needs at a lower price.</p>
    </div>

